  "comments": [],
  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A healthy man could be dead by supper. England, 1485, and a new sickness was moving faster than any plague.",
        "visual_query": "tudor england village street",
        "visual_fallback": "old village"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It started with shivering, then a drenching sweat. Survivors recovered in a day. Many never got the day.",
        "visual_query": "candlelit tudor sickroom bed",
        "visual_fallback": "sick room"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It struck the young and strong hardest. Two teenage brothers, dukes of Suffolk, died within an hour of each other. Why would a disease pick the healthy?",
        "visual_query": "tudor manor house interior",
        "visual_fallback": "stone manor"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Five epidemics came and went across seventy years. After 1551 it never returned anywhere, ever again.",
        "visual_query": "16th century english churchyard",
        "visual_fallback": "old churchyard"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The leading modern answer is a hantavirus carried by rodents, argued in a 2014 study from the disease's speed and incubation pattern. No sample of it survives to test, so the sweat keeps both its name and its secret.",
        "visual_query": "field mouse in grain barn",
        "visual_fallback": "barn mice"
      }
    ],
